===== CSV Router

This router is used to route CSV (comma separated values) files to a specified target database table.
The base name of the file is used as the table name, which can be changed using <<Transforms>>.

ifdef::pro[]

image::images/csv-router.png[]

endif::pro[]

ifdef::pro[]
.Required Fields
endif::pro[]

Sync Mode:: Specify whether data is overwritten or appended.
.Sync Mode
[cols="1,3"]
|===

|Full Refresh Overwrite|Send the entire file as relational data and overwrite the target table by clearing it first.  The SQL command to clear the table can overridden with the initial.load.delete.first.sql parameter.

|Full Refresh Append|Send the entire file as relational data and append the target table.

|Incremental Refresh Append|Send lines from the file that are new as relational data and append the target table.

|===
Standardize Names:: Convert the file name and column names to standard SQL names, using lower case and underscores instead of spaces.
Send Table Definition:: Send the table definition (DDL) to create the table if it's missing or alter an existing table to match.
Record Transaction ID:: Record a transaction ID when the file is converted to relational data, so the data will be routed into the same batch.

ifndef::pro[]

.The following SQL statement defines a router that will send data from a CSV file to table1 in the 'corp' node group.
[source, SQL]
----
insert into SYM_ROUTER (router_id, target_table_name,
	source_node_group_id, target_node_group_id, router_type,
	router_expression, create_time, last_update_time) values
	('store-2-corp-csv','table1','store', 'corp', 'csv', 
	'OVERWRITE=true,SEND_DDL=true,STANDARDIZE_NAMES=true,INCLUDE_TRANSACTION_ID=true', current_timestamp, current_timestamp); 
----

endif::pro[]

The first line of the file should list the column names for the table.
For incremental refresh with a new file, all lines after the first one are routed as inserts into the table.
For incremental refresh with a modified file, all lines after the last line read are routed as inserts into the table.
The last line read is recorded to the <<CONTEXT>> table.

IMPORTANT: The CSV Router routes from file to database so a file trigger must be created to specify the directory or path that the file trigger should watch. (see <<File Triggers>>)


